        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Water supply and sewerage have different responsibilities",
        "content": "Water supply and sanitation are closely related, but their coverage measures describe different things. The WHO/UNICEF Joint Monitoring Programme, known as JMP, evaluates drinking water through accessibility, availability and quality. Its sanitation framework follows the management of human excreta through containment, removal where necessary, treatment and final disposal or reuse. A water connection therefore does not establish that a household’s wastewater is safely managed. [s2][s3]\n\nFor Iran, UNICEF identifies aging infrastructure, insufficient maintenance and water scarcity as pressures on communities, with particular consequences for vulnerable children. Its programme works with the Ministry of Energy and water and wastewater companies, alongside education, health and migration authorities. This describes a cross-sector public-health task rather than a responsibility confined to underground pipes. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "energy-networks",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Gas and electricity form a connected energy system",
        "content": "Iran’s gas and electricity systems need to be understood together. The US Energy Information Administration’s October 2024 country analysis describes natural gas as the principal fuel for electricity generation and documents its extensive domestic pipeline system. Residential and commercial gas demand increases in winter, while power-sector gas use rises in summer. The report identifies insufficient storage as a difficulty in balancing seasonal demand. [s5]\n\nThe same analysis distinguishes installed generating capacity from capacity actually available to serve customers. Aging plants and fuel constraints can reduce usable supply; large energy resources do not by themselves guarantee uninterrupted electricity. The report also describes hydropower’s exposure to drought and water shortages. These are conditions documented in that historical assessment, rather than a verified account of every network’s operating position at the research cutoff. [s5]\n\nFor readers, the practical implication is to separate fuel availability, electricity production and delivery to customers. Adding a generating plant, expanding a gas field and improving the reliability of a local distribution network address different parts of the system.",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "communications-and-management",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connectivity and coordination",
        "content": "Communication infrastructure also requires more than a coverage map. The Internet Society’s resilience framework distinguishes physical infrastructure, market readiness, performance and security. Its Iran profile examines such features as mobile connectivity, internet exchange points and diversity among upstream providers. This framework helps explain why the existence of a network and the quality of the service experienced by users require separate evidence. [s6]\n\nAcross utilities, coordination matters because weaknesses can cross sector boundaries. UNICEF’s August 2026 emergency response in Iran explicitly included equipment intended to maintain water disinfection during power disruptions and pumps for wastewater management. This is concrete evidence of a water–energy dependency, although a report of equipment delivery does not establish nationwide service restoration. [s7]\n\nThe most useful overall assessment therefore combines network extent with operational evidence: continuity, quality, maintenance and institutional follow-through. The linked pages examine individual systems; a complete contemporary comparison still requires consistent operator data and a verified account of responsibilities across all utility sectors.",
        "source_ids": ["s6", "s7"]
      }
    ],
