    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Urban water networks connect water sources, treatment, storage and distribution to household services. Evidence from Iranian cities shows why widespread connections must be assessed separately from pressure, water quality, losses and continuity.",
    "tags": ["Urban water", "Drinking water", "Distribution networks", "Water quality", "Ahwaz", "Shiraz"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T13:46:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T13:46:54Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Urban Water Networks in Iran: Connections, Quality and Reliability",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s urban water networks through service definitions, historical evidence from Ahwaz and Shiraz, and maintenance challenges."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "what-network-service-means",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A connection is one part of water service",
        "content": "An urban water network is best understood as a service chain rather than simply a collection of pipes. Historical planning for Ahwaz and Shiraz combined treatment and storage with transmission and distribution works. These components addressed different problems: obtaining treatable water, moving it into the city and maintaining adequate supply within distribution zones. [s1][s2]\n\nThe WHO/UNICEF Joint Monitoring Programme, or JMP, provides a useful standard for interpreting access claims. Safely managed drinking water must come from an improved source, be accessible on the premises, be available when needed and be free from contamination. Improved sources include more than piped connections, so this indicator is not a direct measure of municipal pipe-network coverage. Likewise, a reported connection does not establish that every service criterion has been met. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "different-city-problems",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Ahwaz and Shiraz: different constraints",
        "content": "The January 2004 environmental assessment for the water and sanitation project described Ahwaz, in Khuzestan province, as facing serious source-water pollution. Municipal and industrial discharges, agricultural drainage and solid waste affected the Karoun River. The assessment discussed blending river water with well water and examining additional treatment techniques. These are historical findings about the project’s starting conditions, not a current drinking-water advisory. [s1]\n\nShiraz, in Fars province, presented a different combination of concerns. The project completion report described extensive water-network access at appraisal, alongside distribution losses, insufficient pressure in some zones and quality concerns affecting some groundwater sources. It also explained why treatment, storage and network capacity were included in planning for future demand. High connection coverage therefore coexisted with substantial engineering needs. [s2]\n\nThe comparison matters because a single national access figure cannot identify which intervention a particular city requires. Source protection, treatment improvements and distribution rehabilitation respond to different failures.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "losses-and-evaluation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Measure performance after construction",
        "content": "The independent project review found contrasting results for the historical measure called unaccounted-for water: performance surpassed the target in Shiraz but worsened in Ahwaz. It also reported incomplete reservoir works at project closure. These findings caution against assuming that equipment purchases or civil works automatically produce equivalent improvements everywhere. The historical terminology is retained here; no modern standardized water-loss series has been reconstructed from it. [s4]\n\nUNICEF’s Iran programme identifies aging infrastructure and insufficient maintenance as continuing concerns. Its August 2026 emergency report also describes solar-powered disinfection equipment intended to protect water safety during electricity disruptions. Together, these sources show why renewal and operational preparedness belong alongside expansion in discussions of water infrastructure. They do not establish current pressure, quality or continuity for any individual neighborhood. [s5][s6]\n\nA sound assessment of an urban water network should consequently keep access, availability and quality distinct, then seek local operational evidence for each. Current city-level records remain necessary before this historical and methodological account can become a comprehensive contemporary profile.",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4", "s5", "s6"]
      }
    ],
